## sn1.65
sn1.65:1.1 | S: “What binds the world? | T: “With what is the world placed in bondage?
sn1.65:1.2 | S: How does it travel about? | T: What is its exploration?
sn1.65:1.3 | S: With the giving up of what | T: With the abandoning of what
sn1.65:1.4 | S: are all bonds severed?” | T: are all bonds broken?”
sn1.65:2.1 | S: “Delight binds the world. | T: “With delight is the world placed in bondage.
sn1.65:2.2 | S: It travels about by means of thought. | T: Directed thought is its exploration.
sn1.65:2.3 | S: With the giving up of craving | T: With the abandoning of craving
sn1.65:2.4 | S: all bonds are severed.” | T: all bonds are broken.”

## sn1.69
sn1.69:1.1 | S: “What is it that binds the world? | T: “With what is the world tied down?
sn1.69:1.2 | S: By removing what is it freed? | T: With the subduing of what is it freed?
sn1.69:1.3 | S: With the giving up of what | T: With the abandoning of what
sn1.69:1.4 | S: are all bonds severed?” | T: are all bonds cut through?”
sn1.69:2.1 | S: “Desire is what binds the world. | T: The Buddha: “With desire the world is tied down.
sn1.69:2.2 | S: By the removing of desire it’s freed. | T: With the subduing of desire it’s freed.
sn1.69:2.3 | S: With the giving up of desire, | T: With the abandoning of desire
sn1.69:2.4 | S: all bonds are severed.” | T: all bonds are cut through.”

## sn1.73
sn1.73:1.1 | S: “What’s a person’s best wealth? | T: “What is a person’s best wealth here?
sn1.73:1.2 | S: What brings happiness when practiced well? | T: What, well practiced, brings bliss?
sn1.73:1.3 | S: What’s the sweetest taste of all? | T: What’s the most excellent of savors?
sn1.73:1.4 | S: The one they say has the best life: how do they live?” | T: Living in what way is one’s life called the best?”
sn1.73:2.1 | S: “Faith here is a person’s best wealth. | T: “Conviction is a person’s best wealth here.
sn1.73:2.2 | S: The teaching brings happiness when practiced well. | T: Dhamma, well practiced, brings bliss.
sn1.73:2.3 | S: Truth is the sweetest taste of all. | T: Truth is the most excellent of savors.
sn1.73:2.4 | S: The one they say has the best life lives by wisdom.” | T: Living with discernment, one’s life is called best.”

## sn11.22
sn11.22:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn11.22:1.2 | P: Tatra kho …pe… etadavoca: | T: (none)
sn11.22:1.3 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, there was a native spirit who was ugly and deformed. He sat on the throne of Sakka, lord of gods. | T: “Once, monks, a certain yakkha—ugly & misshapen—sat down on Sakka the deva-king’s seat.
sn11.22:1.4 | S: But the gods of the thirty-three complained, grumbled, and objected, | T: Then the devas of the Thirty-three were indignant, annoyed, & complained:
sn11.22:1.5 | S: ‘Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: ‘Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding!
sn11.22:1.6 | S: This ugly and deformed spirit is sitting on the throne of Sakka, the lord of gods.’ | T: This yakkha—ugly & misshapen—has sat down on Sakka’s seat!’
sn11.22:1.7 | S: But the more the gods complained, the more attractive, good-looking, and lovely that spirit became. | T: But the more the devas of the Thirty-three were indignant, annoyed, & complained, the more well-formed that yakkha became, the more good-looking & inspiring.
sn11.22:2.1 | S: So the gods went up to Sakka and told him what had happened. He replied, | T: “Then the devas of the Thirty-three went to Sakka the deva-king and, on arrival, said to him,
sn11.22:2.2 | P: ‘idha te, mārisa, aññataro yakkho dubbaṇṇo okoṭimako sakkassa devānamindassa āsane nisinno. | T: ‘Just now, dear sir, a certain yakkha—ugly & misshapen—sat down on your seat.
sn11.22:2.3 | P: Tatra sudaṁ, mārisa, devā tāvatiṁsā ujjhāyanti khiyyanti vipācenti: | T: The devas of the Thirty-three were indignant, annoyed, & complained:
sn11.22:2.4 | P: “acchariyaṁ vata bho, abbhutaṁ vata bho. | T: “Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding!
sn11.22:2.5 | P: Ayaṁ yakkho dubbaṇṇo okoṭimako sakkassa devānamindassa āsane nisinno”ti. | T: This yakkha—ugly & misshapen—has sat down on Sakka’s seat!”
sn11.22:2.6 | P: Yathā yathā kho, mārisa, devā ujjhāyanti khiyyanti vipācenti tathā tathā so yakkho abhirūpataro ceva hoti dassanīyataro ca pāsādikataro cāti. | T: But the more the devas of the Thirty-three were indignant, annoyed, & complained, the more well-formed that yakkha became, the more good-looking & inspiring.’
sn11.22:2.7 | S: ‘Surely, good fellow, that must be the anger-eating spirit!’ | T: “‘Then, dear sirs, he must be an anger-eating yakkha.’
sn11.22:3.1 | S: Then Sakka went up to that spirit, arranged his robe over one shoulder, knelt on his right knee, raised his joined palms toward the anger-eating spirit, and pronounced his name three times: | T: “So Sakka the deva-king approached the anger-eating yakkha and, on arrival, arranging his upper robe over one shoulder & kneeling with one knee on the ground, raised his hands palm-to-palm over his heart toward the anger-eating yakkha and announced his name three times,
sn11.22:3.2 | S: ‘Good fellow, I am Sakka, lord of gods! Good fellow, I am Sakka, the lord of gods!’ | T: ‘I, dear sir, am Sakka the deva-king! I, dear sir, am Sakka the deva-king! I, dear sir, am Sakka the deva-king!’
sn11.22:3.3 | S: But the more Sakka pronounced his name, the uglier and more deformed the spirit became, | T: The more Sakka the deva-king announced his name, the uglier & more misshapen the yakkha became.
sn11.22:3.4 | S: until eventually it vanished right there. | T: Then, having become uglier & more misshapen, he disappeared right there.
sn11.22:3.5 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, guiding the gods of the thirty-three, spoke this verse: | T: “Then Sakka the deva-king, sitting down on his seat & conciliating the devas of the Thirthy-three, on that occasion recited these verses:
sn11.22:4.1 | S: ‘My mind isn’t easily upset; | T: “‘I’m not easily upset in mind,
sn11.22:4.2 | S: I’m not easily drawn into the maelstrom. | T: nor easily led into a whirl.
sn11.22:4.3 | S: I don’t get angry for long, | T: I don’t get angry for long.
sn11.22:4.4 | S: anger doesn’t last in me. | T: Anger doesn’t persist in me.
sn11.22:5.1 | S: When I do get angry I don’t speak harshly, | T: When angry, I don’t speak harshly
sn11.22:5.2 | S: nor do I advertise my own virtues. | T: or insist on my virtues.
sn11.22:5.3 | S: I carefully restrain myself | T: I keep myself well under control
sn11.22:5.4 | S: out of regard for my own welfare.’” | T: with an eye to my own good.’”

## sn12.10
sn12.10:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, before my awakening—when I was still unawakened but intent on awakening—I thought: | T: “Monks, before my self-awakening, when I was still just an unawakened bodhisatta, the realization came to me:
sn12.10:1.2 | S: ‘Alas, this world has fallen into trouble. It’s born, grows old, dies, passes away, and is reborn, | T: ‘How this world has fallen on difficulty! It is born, it ages, it dies, it falls away & rearises,
sn12.10:1.3 | S: yet it doesn’t understand how to escape from this suffering, from old age and death. | T: but it doesn’t discern the escape from this stress, from this aging & death.
sn12.10:1.4 | S: Oh, when will an escape be found from this suffering, from old age and death?’ | T: O when will it discern the escape from this stress, from this aging & death?’
sn12.10:2.1 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: “Then the thought occurred to me,
sn12.10:2.2 | S: ‘When what exists is there old age and death? What is a requirement for old age and death?’ | T: ‘Aging-&-death exists when what exists? From what as a requisite condition comes aging-&-death?’
sn12.10:2.3 | S: Then, through rational application of mind, I penetrated with wisdom: | T: From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment:
sn12.10:2.4 | S: ‘When rebirth exists there’s old age and death. Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death.’ | T: ‘Aging-&-death exists when birth exists. From birth as a requisite condition comes aging-&-death.’
sn12.10:3.1 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: Then the thought occurred to me,
sn12.10:3.2 | S: ‘When what exists is there rebirth? … | T: ‘Birth exists when what exists? From what as a requisite condition comes birth?’ From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment: ‘Birth exists when becoming exists. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.’ …
sn12.10:3.3 | S: continued existence … | T: “‘Becoming exists when clinging exists.…
sn12.10:3.4 | S: grasping … | T: “‘Clinging exists when craving exists.…
sn12.10:3.5 | S: craving … | T: “‘Craving exists when feeling exists.…
sn12.10:3.6 | S: feeling … | T: “‘Feeling exists when contact exists.…
sn12.10:3.7 | S: contact … | T: “‘Contact exists when the six sense media exist.…
sn12.10:3.8 | S: the six sense fields … | T: “‘The six sense media exist when name-&-form exists.…
sn12.10:3.9 | S: name and form … | T: “‘Name-&-form exists when consciousness exists.…
sn12.10:3.10 | S: consciousness … | T: “‘Consciousness exists when fabrications exist.…
sn12.10:3.11 | S: ‘When what exists are there choices? What is a requirement for choices?’ | T: Then the thought occurred to me, ‘Fabrications exist when what exists? From what as a requisite condition come fabrications?’
sn12.10:3.12 | S: Then, through rational application of mind, I penetrated with wisdom: | T: From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment:
sn12.10:3.13 | S: ‘When ignorance exists there are choices. Ignorance is a requirement for choices.’ | T: ‘Fabrications exist when ignorance exists. From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.10:4.1 | S: And so, ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “‘Thus: From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.10:4.2 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.10:4.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.10:4.4 | S: ‘Origination, origination.’ Such was the vision, knowledge, wisdom, realization, and light that arose in me regarding teachings not learned before from another. | T: Origination, origination.’ Vision arose, clear knowing arose, discernment arose, knowledge arose, illumination arose within me with regard to things never before heard.
sn12.10:5.1 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: “Then the thought occurred to me,
sn12.10:5.2 | S: ‘When what doesn’t exist is there no old age and death? When what ceases do old age and death cease?’ | T: ‘Aging & death don’t exist when what doesn’t exist? From the cessation of what comes the cessation of aging & death?’
sn12.10:5.3 | S: Then, through rational application of mind, I penetrated with wisdom: | T: From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment:
sn12.10:5.4 | S: ‘When rebirth doesn’t exist there’s no old age and death. When rebirth ceases, old age and death cease.’ | T: ‘Aging-&-death doesn’t exist when birth doesn’t exist. From the cessation of birth comes the cessation of aging & death.’
sn12.10:6.1 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: “Then the thought occurred to me,
sn12.10:6.2 | S: ‘When what doesn’t exist is there no rebirth? … | T: ‘Birth doesn’t exist when what doesn’t exist? From the cessation of what comes the cessation of birth?’ From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment: ‘Birth doesn’t exist when becoming doesn’t exist. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth.’ …
sn12.10:6.3 | S: continued existence … | T: “‘Becoming doesn’t exist when clinging doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.4 | S: grasping … | T: “‘Clinging doesn’t exist when craving doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.5 | S: craving … | T: “‘Craving doesn’t exist when feeling doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.6 | S: feeling … | T: “‘Feeling doesn’t exist when contact doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.7 | S: contact … | T: “‘Contact doesn’t exist when the six sense media don’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.8 | S: the six sense fields … | T: “‘The six sense media don’t exist when name-&-form doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.9 | S: name and form … | T: “‘Name-&-form doesn’t exist when consciousness doesn’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.10 | S: consciousness … | T: “‘Consciousness doesn’t exist when fabrications don’t exist.…
sn12.10:6.11 | S: ‘When what doesn’t exist are there no choices? When what ceases do choices cease?’ | T: Then the thought occurred to me, ‘Fabrications don’t exist when what doesn’t exist? From the cessation of what comes the cessation of fabrications?’
sn12.10:6.12 | S: Then, through rational application of mind, I penetrated with wisdom: | T: From my appropriate attention there came the breakthrough of discernment:
sn12.10:6.13 | S: ‘When ignorance doesn’t exist there are no choices. When ignorance ceases, choices cease.’ | T: ‘Fabrications don’t exist when ignorance doesn’t exist. From the cessation of ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.10:7.1 | S: And so, when ignorance ceases, choices cease. | T: “‘Thus: From the cessation of ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.10:7.2 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.10:7.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.10:7.4 | S: ‘Cessation, cessation.’ Such was the vision, knowledge, wisdom, realization, and light that arose in me regarding teachings not learned before from another.” | T: Cessation, cessation.’ Vision arose, clear knowing arose, discernment arose, knowledge arose, illumination arose within me with regard to things never before heard.”
sn12.10:7.5 | P: Dasamo. | T: (none)
sn12.10:7.6 | P: Buddhavaggo paṭhamo. | T: (none)
sn12.10:8.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn12.10:8.1 | P: Desanā vibhaṅgapaṭipadā ca, | T: (none)
sn12.10:8.2 | P: Vipassī sikhī ca vessabhū; | T: (none)
sn12.10:8.3 | P: Kakusandho koṇāgamano kassapo, | T: (none)
sn12.10:8.4 | P: Mahāsakyamuni ca gotamoti. | T: (none)

## sn12.11
sn12.11:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn12.11:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. … | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn12.11:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, these four fuels maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: There he addressed the monks, “Monks, there are these four nutriments for the maintenance of beings who have come into being or for the support of those in search of a place to be born.
sn12.11:1.4 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
sn12.11:1.5 | S: Edible food, whether solid or subtle; contact is the second, mental intention the third, and consciousness the fourth. | T: Physical food, gross or refined; contact as the second; intellectual intention the third; and consciousness the fourth.
sn12.11:1.6 | S: These are the four fuels that maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: These are the four nutriments for the maintenance of beings who have come into being or for the support of those in search of a place to be born.
sn12.11:2.1 | S: What is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of these four fuels? | T: “Now, these four nutriments have what as their cause, what as their origination, what as their source, what as that which brings them into play?
sn12.11:2.2 | S: Craving. | T: These four nutriments have craving as their cause, craving as their origination, craving as their source, craving as that which brings them into play.
sn12.11:2.3 | S: And what is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of craving? | T: “And this craving has what as its cause, what as its origination, what as its source, what as that which brings it into play? …
sn12.11:2.4 | S: Feeling. | T: Feeling.…
sn12.11:2.5 | S: And what is the source of feeling? | T: “And this feeling has what as its cause…? …
sn12.11:2.6 | S: Contact. | T: Contact.…
sn12.11:2.7 | S: And what is the source of contact? | T: “And this contact has what as its cause…? …
sn12.11:2.8 | S: The six sense fields. | T: The six sense media.…
sn12.11:2.9 | S: And what is the source of the six sense fields? | T: “And these six sense media have what as their cause…? …
sn12.11:2.10 | S: Name and form. | T: Name-&-form.…
sn12.11:2.11 | S: And what is the source of name and form? | T: “And this name-&-form has what as its cause…? …
sn12.11:2.12 | S: Consciousness. | T: Consciousness.…
sn12.11:2.13 | S: And what is the source of consciousness? | T: “And this consciousness has what as its cause…? …
sn12.11:2.14 | S: Choices. | T: Fabrication.…
sn12.11:2.15 | S: And what is the source of choices? | T: “And this fabrication has what as its cause, what as its origination, what as its source, what as that which brings it into play?
sn12.11:2.16 | S: Ignorance. | T: Fabrication has ignorance as its cause, ignorance as its origination, ignorance as its source, ignorance as that which brings it into play.
sn12.11:3.1 | S: And so, mendicants, ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “Thus, from ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.11:3.2 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: “From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. “From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. “From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. “From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. “From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. “From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. “From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. “From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. “From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. “From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.11:3.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.11:3.4 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.11:3.5 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.11:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.”
sn12.11:3.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.15
sn12.15:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī …
sn12.15:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Kaccānagotta went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Kaccāna Gotta went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
sn12.15:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘right view’. | T: “Lord, ‘Right view, right view,’ it is said.
sn12.15:1.4 | S: How is right view defined?” | T: To what extent is there right view?”
sn12.15:2.1 | S: “Kaccāna, this world mostly relies on the duality of existence and non-existence. | T: “By & large, Kaccāna, this world is supported by [takes as its object] a polarity, that of existence & non-existence.
sn12.15:2.2 | S: But for one who truly sees the origin of the world with right understanding, the concept of non-existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: But when one sees the origination of the world as it has come to be with right discernment, ‘non-existence’ with reference to the world does not occur to one.
sn12.15:2.3 | S: And for one who truly sees the cessation of the world with right understanding, the concept of existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: When one sees the cessation of the world as it has come to be with right discernment, ‘existence’ with reference to the world does not occur to one.
sn12.15:2.4 | S: This world is for the most part shackled by getting involved, grasping, and insisting. | T: “By & large, Kaccāna, this world is in bondage to attachments, clingings [sustenances], & biases.
sn12.15:2.5 | S: But if, when it comes to this getting involved & grasping, mental fixation, and insistence & underlying tendency, they are not attracted, do not grasp, and do not fixate on the thought, “my self”, | T: But one such as this does not get involved with or cling to these attachments, clingings, fixations of awareness, biases, or obsessions; nor is he resolved on ‘my self.’
sn12.15:2.6 | S: they’ll have no doubt or uncertainty that what arises is just suffering arising, and what ceases is just suffering ceasing. Their knowledge of this is independent of others. | T: He has no uncertainty or doubt that mere stress, when arising, is arising; stress, when passing away, is passing away. In this, his knowledge is independent of others.
sn12.15:2.7 | S: This is how right view is defined. | T: It’s to this extent, Kaccāna, that there is right view.
sn12.15:3.1 | S: ‘All exists’: this is one extreme. | T: “‘Everything exists’: That is one extreme.
sn12.15:3.2 | S: ‘All does not exist’: this is the second extreme. | T: ‘Everything doesn’t exist’: That is a second extreme.
sn12.15:3.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Avoiding these two extremes, the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma via the middle:
sn12.15:3.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.15:3.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.15:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.15:3.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.15:3.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.15:3.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.”
sn12.15:3.10 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.17
sn12.17:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn12.17:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn12.17:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Rājagaha for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One, having adjusted his lower robe and taking his bowl & outer robe, went into Rājagaha for alms.
sn12.17:1.4 | S: The naked ascetic Kassapa saw the Buddha coming off in the distance. | T: Kassapa the clothless ascetic saw him coming from afar.
sn12.17:1.5 | S: He went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: On seeing him, he went to him and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn12.17:1.6 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he stood to one side and said to the Buddha, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he stood to one side. As he was standing there, he said to the Blessed One,
sn12.17:1.7 | S: “I’d like to ask the worthy Gotama about a certain point, if you’d take the time to answer.” | T: “We would like to question Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would take the time to answer our question.”
sn12.17:1.8 | S: “Kassapa, it’s the wrong time for questions. | T: “This is not the time for a question, Kassapa.
sn12.17:1.9 | S: We’ve entered an inhabited area.” | T: We have entered among houses.”
sn12.17:2.1 | S: A second time, | T: A second time.…
sn12.17:2.2 | P: “puccheyyāma mayaṁ bhavantaṁ gotamaṁ kañcideva desaṁ, sace no bhavaṁ gotamo okāsaṁ karoti pañhassa veyyākaraṇāyā”ti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.3 | P: “Akālo kho tāva, kassapa, pañhassa; | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.4 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhā”ti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.5 | S: and a third time, Kassapa spoke to the Buddha and the Buddha replied. | T: A third time Kassapa the clothless ascetic said to him, “We would like to question Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would take the time to answer our question.” “This is not the time for a question, Kassapa.
sn12.17:2.6 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhāti. | T: We have entered among houses.”
sn12.17:2.7 | S: When this was said, Kassapa said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Kassapa the clothless ascetic said,
sn12.17:2.8 | S: “I don’t want to ask much.” | T: “What we want to ask isn’t much.”
sn12.17:2.9 | S: “Ask what you wish, Kassapa.” | T: “Then ask as you like.”
sn12.17:3.1 | S: “Well, worthy Gotama, is suffering made by oneself?” | T: “Master Gotama, is pain self-made?”
sn12.17:3.2 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.3 | S: “Then is suffering made by another?” | T: “Then is it other-made?”
sn12.17:3.4 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.5 | S: “Well, is suffering made by both oneself and another?” | T: “Then is it both self-made and other-made?”
sn12.17:3.6 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.7 | S: “Then does suffering arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “Then is it the case that pain, without self-making or other-making, is spontaneously arisen?”
sn12.17:3.8 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.9 | S: “Well, is there no such thing as suffering?” | T: “Then is there no pain?”
sn12.17:3.10 | S: “It’s not that there’s no such thing as suffering. | T: “It’s not the case, Kassapa, that there is no pain.
sn12.17:3.11 | S: Suffering is real.” | T: There is pain.”
sn12.17:3.12 | S: “Then does the worthy Gotama not know or see suffering?” | T: “Then, in that case, does Master Gotama not know or see pain?”

