sn12.17:4.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, when asked these questions, you say ‘not so’. | T: “Now, Master Gotama, when asked, ‘Is pain self-made?’ you say, ‘Don’t say that, Kassapa.’
sn12.17:4.2 | P: ‘Kiṁ pana, bho gotama, paraṅkataṁ dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: When asked, ‘Then is it other-made?‘ you say, ‘Don’t say that, Kassapa.’
sn12.17:4.3 | P: ‘Kiṁ nu kho, bho gotama, sayaṅkatañca paraṅkatañca dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: When asked, ‘Then is it both self-made and other-made?’ you say, ‘Don’t say that, Kassapa.’
sn12.17:4.4 | P: ‘Kiṁ pana, bho gotama, asayaṅkāraṁ aparaṅkāraṁ adhiccasamuppannaṁ dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: When asked, ‘Then is it the case that pain, being neither self-made nor other-made, arises spontaneously?’ you say, ‘Don’t say that, Kassapa.’
sn12.17:4.5 | S: Yet you say that there is such a thing as suffering. | T: When asked, ‘Then is there no pain?’ you say, ‘It’s not the case, Kassapa, that there is no pain. There is pain.‘
sn12.17:4.6 | P: ‘Tena hi bhavaṁ gotamo dukkhaṁ na jānāti na passatī’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘na khvāhaṁ, kassapa, dukkhaṁ na jānāmi na passāmi. | T: When asked, ‘Well, in that case, does Master Gotama not know or see pain?’ you say, ‘Kassapa, it’s not the case that I don’t know or see pain.
sn12.17:4.7 | S: And you say that you do know suffering, | T: I know pain.
sn12.17:4.8 | S: and you do see suffering. | T: I see pain.’
sn12.17:4.9 | S: Sir, let the Blessed One explain suffering to me! | T: Then tell me about pain, lord Blessed One.
sn12.17:4.10 | S: Let the Blessed One teach me about suffering!” | T: Teach me about pain, lord Blessed One!”
sn12.17:5.1 | S: “Suppose that he who does the deed and he who experiences the result are one and the same. Then for one who has existed since the beginning, suffering is made by oneself. In speaking like this, one implies this is eternal. | T: “Kassapa, the statement, ‘With the one who acts being the same as the one who experiences, existing from the beginning, pain is self-made’: This circles around eternalism.
sn12.17:5.2 | S: Suppose that he who does the deed is one, and he who experiences the result is another. Then for one stricken by feeling, suffering is made by another. In speaking like this, one implies this is annihilated. | T: And the statement, ‘With the one who acts being one thing, and the one who experiences being another, existing as the one struck by the feeling, pain is other-made’: This circles around annihilationism.
sn12.17:5.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Avoiding these two extremes, the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma via the middle:
sn12.17:5.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.17:5.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.17:5.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.17:5.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.17:5.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.17:5.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.”
sn12.17:6.1 | S: When this was said, Kassapa said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Kassapa the clothless ascetic said,
sn12.17:6.2 | S: “Excellent, sir! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, lord! Magnificent!
sn12.17:6.3 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, the Buddha has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has the Blessed One—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn12.17:6.4 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to the Blessed One for refuge, to the Dhamma, and to the Saṅgha of monks.
sn12.17:6.5 | S: Sir, may I receive the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence?” | T: Let me obtain the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, let me obtain Acceptance [into the Saṅgha of monks].”
sn12.17:7.1 | S: “Kassapa, if someone formerly ordained in another sect wishes to take the going forth, the ordination in this teaching and training, they must spend four months on probation. When four months have passed, if the mendicants are satisfied, they’ll give the going forth, the ordination into monkhood. | T: “Anyone, Kassapa, who has previously belonged to another sect and who desires the Going-forth & Acceptance in this Dhamma & Vinaya, must first undergo probation for four months. If, at the end of four months, the monks feel so moved, they give him the Going-forth & accept him to the monk’s state.
sn12.17:7.2 | S: However, I have recognized individual differences.” | T: But I know distinctions among individuals in this matter.”
sn12.17:8.1 | S: “Sir, if four months probation are required in such a case, I’ll spend four years on probation. When four years have passed, if the mendicants are satisfied, let them give me the going forth, the ordination into monkhood.” | T: “Lord, if that is so, I am willing to undergo probation for four years. If, at the end of four years, the monks feel so moved, let them give me the going forth & accept me to the monk’s state.”
sn12.17:9.1 | S: And the naked ascetic Kassapa received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: Then Kassapa the clothless ascetic obtained the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, he obtained Acceptance.
sn12.17:9.2 | S: Not long after his ordination, Venerable Kassapa, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
sn12.17:9.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
sn12.17:9.4 | S: And Venerable Kassapa became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Ven. Kassapa became another one of the arahants.”
sn12.17:9.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


